{
  "result": "I've always been fascinated by how much of our lives happens in waiting rooms. Not literal ones with the uncomfortable chairs and old magazines, though those count. I mean the in-between spaces.\n\nYou're waiting for coffee to brew, so you stare at the toaster. You're waiting for a reply to a text, so you refresh your email even though it has nothing to do with it. You're waiting to become the kind of person who wakes up early and jogs, and so you live as the person who thinks about waking up early.\n\nThe other day I watched a guy at the grocery store who was completely absorbed by the self-checkout machine yelling \"unexpected item in bagging area.\" He didn't get frustrated. He just stood there, patient, like he had accepted this was his moment in history. It was kind of beautiful.\n\nWe talk a lot about big moments, but I think we are mostly made out of these tiny pauses. The three seconds you hold a door open for someone who is still too far away, so you both do that awkward half-run. The way you peel the label off a bottle without thinking.\n\nIf you pay attention there, life stops feeling like something you're waiting *for* and starts feeling like something you're *in*. Which, I guess, is the whole trick. Not glamorous, not Instagrammable, just quietly, entirely yours. And honestly, I've come to prefer it that way. It's softer here, stranger, and more true. You should try listening.",
